<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!--
      INTERNET, for the Google APIs first-run setup calls (FR-101, FR-901, FR-106) and, when
      the write path lands, FR-801.

      Until this line existed the app could not reach the network at all, which was the
      strongest possible form of AC-17 ("no outbound request to any non-Google endpoint").
      That guarantee is now a property of the code, not of the manifest, and it rests on two
      things a reviewer has to check rather than one they could take for granted:

        - Every request this app makes goes through GoogleHttp in :data, which refuses any
          host outside an explicit allowlist and does not follow redirects. It is the single
          place outbound traffic can originate, in the same way SetupEffect.Commit is the
          single place a calendar can be created (FR-105).
        - Play services makes its own calls for the OAuth grant. Those go to Google, so
          AC-17 holds, but they are not ours to route and will appear on a network monitor.

      A networkSecurityConfig would not add anything: it constrains trust anchors and
      cleartext, and cannot deny a host. Cleartext is already off by default at this
      targetSdk.

      Deliberately absent, and to stay absent (§5.2):
        - MediaProjection screen capture (FR-217)
        - AccessibilityService (FR-218)
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

    <!--
      NOT DECLARED HERE, BUT PRESENT IN THE MERGED MANIFEST. Adding androidx.work for FR-806's
      write queue brings four permissions in from its own manifest, and they are named here
      because this file is the place a reviewer comes to find out what the app can do. Verify
      with:

        ./gradlew :app:processDebugMainManifest
        app/build/intermediates/merged_manifest/debug/.../AndroidManifest.xml

        - ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE   the connectivity constraint the drain waits on
        - RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED reschedules unfinished work after a restart, which is
                                 literally NFR-302's "device restart while queued"
        - WAKE_LOCK              holds the device awake for the duration of a drain
        - FOREGROUND_SERVICE     WorkManager's expedited path

      None of them widens AC-17: that rests on ALLOWED_HOSTS in GoogleHttp, and every request
      the queue makes goes through it like any other. None of them is a runtime permission, so
      none produces a prompt. FOREGROUND_SERVICE is the one worth watching — NFR-104 forbids a
      persistent background service, and WorkManager satisfies that only so long as work stays
      one-shot and constrained. A periodic worker would be a new NFR-104 decision.
    -->

    <!--
      ML KIT (FR-215) ADDS NO PERMISSION, AND FIVE COMPONENTS. Verified 28 Aug 2026 by merging
      the manifest with and without :ocr and diffing the two — the permission sets are
      byte-identical, so the list above is still the whole list. Reproduce with:

        ./gradlew :app:processDebugMainManifest
        app/build/intermediates/merged_manifest/debug/.../AndroidManifest.xml

      What it does add is recorded here for the same reason the WorkManager permissions are:
      this file is where a reviewer comes to find out what the app can do.

        - MlKitInitProvider              a ContentProvider, so it runs at process start,
                                         BEFORE Application.onCreate. Every launch pays it,
                                         including the text captures that never use OCR.
        - MlKitComponentDiscoveryService a manifest-declared registry of component registrars.
                                         Never started; it exists to be read, not run.
        - datatransport TransportBackendDiscovery,
          JobInfoSchedulerService,
          AlarmManagerSchedulerBroadcastReceiver
                                         ML Kit's logging pipeline. The CctBackendFactory
                                         meta-data names Google's Clearcut transport: this is
                                         what uploads ML Kit's usage telemetry.

      TWO CONSEQUENCES, NEITHER A DEFECT.

      AC-17. This is the concrete form of the restatement recorded in the SRS: a second
      component now makes its own connections outside GoogleHttp's ALLOWED_HOSTS guard. The
      traffic goes to Google, so the criterion holds as written, and it is not ours to route —
      the same standing Play services' OAuth grant has. It WILL appear on a network monitor
      and must not be reported as a failure. Removing these components with tools:node="remove"
      was considered and is not taken: the traffic is inside AC-17, and stripping a library's
      transport is how a crash arrives in a path with no test to catch it.

      NFR-202 holds — ML Kit's telemetry is API usage, latency and error codes, not message or
      image content, and the requirement forbids collecting content. NFR-104 holds too: a
      JobService is scheduled work, the same standing WorkManager already has, not a persistent
      background service.
    -->

    <application
        android:name=".LatchApplication"
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.Latch">

        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.Latch">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!--
          Capture layers 1, 2 and 4 all land here (§5.2). Each works independently of the
          others, so the activity treats the incoming intent as the only source of truth.

          android:label is "Latch" — 5 characters, within the 6 that FR-202 allows for the
          floating text-selection toolbar.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".capture.CaptureActivity"
            android:excludeFromRecents="true"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="@string/capture_action_label"
            android:launchMode="singleTop"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.Latch.Capture">

            <!-- Layer 1: text selection (FR-201). -->
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.PROCESS_TEXT" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <data android:mimeType="text/plain" />
            </intent-filter>

            <!-- Layer 2: share sheet (FR-205). -->
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.SEND" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <data android:mimeType="text/plain" />
            </intent-filter>

            <!--
              FR-205 also requires image/* and FR-207 application/pdf. Those filters are not
              declared yet: on-device OCR (FR-215) is not implemented, and a share target
              that accepts a file it cannot read is worse than one that does not appear.
              They go in with the OCR work.
            -->
        </activity>

        <!--
          Layer 4: Quick Settings tile (FR-213). The supported path for apps where partial
          text selection is unavailable and the user has to use Copy. The clipboard is read
          by the foreground activity, never by the tile — background clipboard access has
          been blocked since Android 10 (FR-214, §8.4).
        -->
        <service
            android:name=".capture.CaptureTileService"
            android:exported="true"
            android:icon="@android:drawable/ic_menu_today"
            android:label="@string/capture_action_label"
            android:permission="android.permission.BIND_QUICK_SETTINGS_TILE">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.service.quicksettings.action.QS_TILE" />
            </intent-filter>
        </service>

    </application>

</manifest>
